# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever `core.autocrlf` happens to be
# on the machine you cloned onto.
#
# Every file in this repo's index is LF and always has been. The build tools
# (blog/build-site.mjs, blog/build.mjs) write LF too. With core.autocrlf=true
# and no rules, git wanted the working tree to be CRLF instead, so every build
# produced "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" and the
# tree drifted into a mix (31 LF / 14 CRLF / 1 mixed as of 2026-07-26).
#
# Pinning eol=lf changes no content -- the index was already LF -- it just
# stops git and the generators disagreeing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touch photos or fonts.
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
